#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# for_all

#% text
# `for_all(x, formula)` is the statement that every `x` satisfies the formula; `for_all([x, y], formula)` binds several variables, `for_all(x, cond, formula)` is `for_all(x, implies(cond, formula))`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `for_all` stays as written; `resolve`, `reduce` and `cylindrical_decomposition` eliminate it.

#% item
# Variables that do not occur are dropped, and a `for_all` over no variable is its formula.

#% item
# `not(for_all(x, f))` is `exists(x, not f)`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# An unresolved statement:

#% cell
for_all([x, y], x^2 + y^2 >= 0)
#% output
#= for_all([x, y], x^2 + y^2 >= 0)

#% text
# Resolved:

#% cell
resolve(for_all([x, y], x^2 + y^2 >= 0))
#% output
#= true

#% text
# Boolean variables:

#% cell
resolve(for_all([p, q], implies(p and implies(p, q), q)))
#% output
#= true

#% text
# When is a quadratic positive everywhere:

#% cell
reduce(for_all(x, x^2 + a*x + 1 > 0), a)
#% output
#= -2 < a and a < 2

#% text
# Negation:

#% cell
not(for_all(x, x == a))
#% output
#= exists(x, x != a)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `resolve`, `exists`, `cylindrical_decomposition`, `reduce`, `find_instance`
